# Example: calling C code through the generated wrapper (roadmap §3.1).
#
# Pipeline:
#   1. Scan a C header:        python3 tools/cpp_scan.py mylib.h --emit-idl mylib.def
#   2. Generate bindings:      python3 tools/bindgen.py mylib.def --language c --out mylib_wrap.h
#   3. Implement the wrapper against your library, build a shared object
#      (native modules require the capability sandbox, roadmap §2.2).
#   4. Call it from Inimerse through a `native` module declaration:
#
# native mylib {
#     fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32
# }
#
# Functions whose signatures are not convertible (function pointers,
# pointer-to-scalar, structs by value) are reported as manual adaptation
# points by cpp_scan instead of being silently wrapped.
say "see tools/cpp_scan.py --help and docs/ROADMAP_0.5-0.6.md §3.1"
